  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nEarly morning upper air analysis showed a highly-amplified omega\nblocking pattern over the lower 48 states, anchored by a closed\nlow over California and longwave trough over the eastern\nseaboard. Between these features, an upper level ridge extended\nfrom northern Mexico, through much of the Great Plains and\nMidwest.\n\nAs expected with an Omega block, this pattern will be slow to\nevolve over the next several days. The California closed low will\nlift into the 4-corners region by this weekend, displacing the\nupper ridge to the lower and middle Mississippi River Valley.\n500mb heights over the Midsouth will peak near 590dam Sunday\nafternoon. This will result in continued above normal high\ntemperatures (5 degrees on average) and no chances of rainfall.\nAbout half of the Midsouth experienced a one class degradation in\ndrought classification this week, with D3-D4 (extreme to\nexceptional) drought now encompassing most of eastern Arkansas\nand southern West Tennessee. The combination of dry vegetation\nand low daytime humidity will result in continued elevated\nwildland fire danger through the middle of next week. The\npersistent upper level ridge will at least result in light\nsurface winds through the weekend, mitigating the tendency for\nwildfires to rapidly spread.\n\nBeyond this weekend, the western U.S. upper low will deamplify as\nit lifts northeast to the central plains on Monday. By Tuesday,\nthis feature will incorporate into faster westerly flow over the\nupper Midwest. Any midlevel height reduction over the Midsouth\nwill likely be modest, with minimal change in surface\ntemperatures, humidity and rainfall chances.\n\nMedium range models point toward a return to a more progressive\nupper level pattern by late next week. At these extended periods,\nthere are timing differences with systems coming onshore along\nthe west coast. While it's too early to pin down when our next\nrain chance will be, the transition from a blocking pattern to a\nmore typical progressive mid-autumn pattern does point toward\nmore seasonal rainfall as we enter November.",
